# clinical v1.19 — WARNING. The medication effective dates written under the
# undefined health: spellings.
#
# WHY THIS EXACT RECORD. This is not an invented mistake; it is what the
# reference import path emits today, byte for byte in structure. It types the
# record clinical:Medication and then writes the effective period to
# health:startDate and health:endDate, two predicates the health vocabulary does
# not define and no shape targeted. The result was a record that CARRIES its
# effective period on a predicate validated by nothing, while the declared
# clinical:startDate and clinical:endDate sat unused — the clinical v1.15
# procedureName defect, in two more places.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. It asserts that
# clinical:MedicationDateSpellingShape NOTICES both predicates: two sh:Warning
# results, one per spelling. It asserts equally that NOTHING REJECTS the record.
# That second half is the whole compatibility claim of the release: unlike
# clinical:procedureName, whose shape required a name and therefore needed an
# sh:or window to stop failing records that carried one, these dates are
# OPTIONAL at the shape level. No pod fails today for carrying the health:
# spelling and none will. The warning is the entire migration window.
#
# The record carries NO clinical: date, which is what a converter that has not
# yet been changed produces. A record carrying both spellings would warn too,
# but it would not be evidence about the converter's current output.
#
# THIS FIXTURE IS WHAT CLOSES THE WINDOW. clinical v1.19 says the spelling
# shapes are removed once the warning is observably absent from conforming
# output. Until the cascade-cli converter writes the clinical: spellings, this
# fixture warns; when the window closes it must be retired in the same commit
# that removes the shape, and its continuing to warn is the record that the
# window is still open.
#
# Fully synthetic. Same drug and dosage as
# medication-dates-lisinopril.VALID.ttl, differing in the predicate and nothing
# else, so the two records are comparable term for term.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix clinical: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/clinical/v1#> .
@prefix health: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/health/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:med0119-0000-4000-8000-000000000002> a clinical:Medication ;
    clinical:drugName "Lisinopril" ;
    clinical:dosage "20 mg" ;
    clinical:frequency "once daily" ;
    clinical:route "oral" ;
    clinical:status "completed" ;
    health:startDate "2024-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    health:endDate "2025-01-15T09:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    clinical:rxNormCode <http://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/rxnorm/197884> ;
    clinical:prescriber "Dr. Amara Osei" ;
    clinical:sourceEHR "Meridian Health System" ;
    cascade:dataProvenance cascade:EHRVerified ;
    cascade:schemaVersion "1.4" .
